# Limelight — Documentation on Large Changes

When working in `streamer-sim/`, treat `docs/` as part of the deliverable for non-trivial work.

## Read first

- Start from [`streamer-sim/docs/README.md`](streamer-sim/docs/README.md) for the doc map and architecture.
- Docs describe **current implementation** (real numbers from source), not aspirational design.
- [`streamer-sim/IMPROVEMENTS.md`](streamer-sim/IMPROVEMENTS.md) is backlog only — never substitute it for implementation docs.

## When docs are required

Update docs in the **same change** when the work is **large**, meaning any of:

- New game system, mechanic, or player-facing feature
- Formula, economy, resolver, or segment/presence math changes
- LLM stack changes (providers, adapter, prompts, routing, evaluator contract)
- Persistence or save-format changes
- New UI panel, modal, or major workflow change
- Refactor or feature touching **3+ files** in one subsystem (`game/`, `llm/`, `state/`, `ui/`, `persist/`)

Small fixes (typo, single-line bug, CSS tweak, rename with no behavior change) do not need doc updates unless they fix a documented mismatch.

## What to update

1. **Primary doc** — edit the best-fit numbered doc (`docs/01-overview.md` … `docs/09-expectation-vs-reality.md`) with what the code actually does: formulas, thresholds, file map, flow.
2. **Index** — if you add a new doc or rename scope, update `docs/README.md`.
3. **Expectation vs reality** — if you fix UI text, comments, or behavior that contradicted docs (or docs that lied about code), update `docs/09-expectation-vs-reality.md`.
4. **Overview / file map** — if you add modules or move orchestration, update `docs/01-overview.md`.

## Definition of done

A large change is **not complete** until the relevant `docs/` files are updated. Include doc edits in the same commit/PR as the code unless the user explicitly defers documentation.

## Style

- Prefer updating existing sections over new sprawling files.
- Quote real constants and file paths from source; avoid vague “handles X” prose.
- Keep the golden rule visible where relevant: **LLM proposes flavor/classification; code owns numbers** (`resolver.ts`, `controller.ts`).
